            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest notable Perseverance rover news is:\n\n- **Perseverance has driven a “marathon” on Mars.** NASA says the rover reached **26.2 miles / 42.195 km** of total driving on **Sol 1,890**, after about **five years and four months** on the Red Planet. That is much faster than Opportunity, which took over 11 years to reach the same distance. A Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter/HiRISE image also captured the rover and its tracks from orbit near a region nicknamed **“Arbot”**【6:0†source】.\n\n- **NASA recently highlighted new science at “Broom Point.”** Perseverance has been examining layered bedrock on the rim of Jezero Crater, described as likely **more than 3.9 billion years old**. NASA reports the rover found evidence that a roughly **245-foot / 75-meter-thick** stack of ancient rock on the crater rim was built by repeated asteroid impacts【6:1†source】.\n\n- **The rover continues exploring beyond Jezero Crater’s western rim.** NASA’s recent Perseverance updates include route maps, panoramas, and a sweeping view of “Broom Point,” showing the rover’s ongoing campaign in the ancient terrain west of Jezero【6:1†source】.\n\nIn short: Perseverance is still active, has just passed a major distance milestone, and is now studying very old crater-rim rocks that may preserve evidence of Mars’ early impact and environmental history.",
                "annotations": [
